Let me see if I understand it.  ;-)

There is a new glsdict.tex that defines some dazzling macros and then
defines the acronyms using \newacronym.  This takes three arguments,
the tag used to select the acronym (e.g., "cas"), the printed form of the
acronym (e.g., "CAS"), and the full version (e.g., "compare-and-swap").
With this definition, \acr{cas} prints "CAS", \IXacr{cas} prints
"compare-and-swap (CAS)" with italics, and \IXacrf{cas} does the same,
but without italics.  Presumably the first \acr{cas} would include the
expansion of the acronym?  It appears that \glsunset{cas} prevents the
first occurrence from including the expansion.

In my younger days, I might have objected to the tag and the printed
form being different, but as my fingers get older, they do appreciate
fewer trips to the SHIFT key.  ;-)

The "compare-and-swap" is interesting.  If the first use was (say)
"\acr{cas} operation", this would be correct.  However, if the first
use was instead "Use a \acr{cas} to update \co{foo}", the hyphens
would no longer be correct.

I have no reasonable ideas on how to handle this.  One possibly
unreasonable idea would be to have a fourth argument to the
\newacronym{} macro, and use something like \acrmod{} to select
the modifier form, that is, the one having the hyphens.  Automatically
removing the hyphens gets complicated.  For example:

	Use a compare-and-swap (CAS) operation!
	The only reasonable choice is compare and swap (CAS).
	We use read-copy update (RCU).
	We use the read-copy-update (RCU) synchronization mechanism.

English!!!  :-/

It might be necessary to tweak the definition depending on the
type of first use.  Sounds painful, though.

Thoughts?

> I know I need to prepare documentation on how to annotate, which
> I have not done yet.
> 
> Almost all the annotations are done by macros defined in
> the preamble.  There are a variety of slightly different macros
> such as \IX{}, \IXh{}{}, \IXalt{}{}, and so on.
> For acronyms, there are \acr{}, \acrpl{}, \Acr{}, \Acrpl{} ...

I am guessing that \acrpl{} is for plural, and that capitalizing the first
letter as in \Acr{} is for when the acronym is the first word in a sentence.

> To add index annotations for acronyms, use \IXacr{}, \IXacrpl{}, etc.

I am lost on \IXh{}, but many of them are documented at their definition.
Oh, does \IXh{} make this sort of index entry?

	Capacity miss, see Cache miss, capacity

Oh, this is the "hierarchical index list" you mention below, got it.

And you can index within epigraphs, it appears.  For example, in
future/fp.tex:

\epigraph{The curious failure of \IX{functional programming} for parallel
	  applications.}
         {\emph{Malte Skarupke}}

> My idea is to tweak definitions of these macros if there need
> some changes in their appearance, if ever.
> 
> Acronyms are defined in glsdict.tex.
> First use of an acronym is expanded to "\emph{long name} (SHORTNAME)".
> Later uses are expanded to "SHORTNAME" only.
> Long name can be extracted by \acrl{} and their variants.
> If first use form is preferable in a later position,
> you can use \acrfst{} and its friends. 

Ah, that could be helpful.

